---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { arc, education } from '../data/skills';

const connect = [
  { label: 'GitHub', handle: '@amanbind007', href: site.socials.github },
  { label: 'LinkedIn', handle: 'in/amanbind', href: site.socials.linkedin },
  { label: 'Goodreads', handle: '/amanbind', href: site.socials.goodreads },
  { label: 'Credly', handle: '6 AWS certs', href: site.socials.credly },
  { label: 'Email', handle: site.emailAlt, href: site.socials.emailAlt },
  { label: 'Email (alt)', handle: site.email, href: site.socials.email },
  { label: 'Résumé', handle: 'PDF', href: site.resumePath },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="How I went from shipping SwiftUI apps to owning release engineering for a 75+ microservice enterprise platform."
>
  <section class="shell pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-24">
    <SectionHeading eyebrow="About" title="The longer version" />

    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-12 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_18rem]">
      <div class="prose-body max-w-2xl">
        <p>
          I'm Aman. I work on infrastructure at TCS in Bengaluru — pipelines,
          deployments, databases, and the machinery that keeps the product
          running.
        </p>

        <h2>Like a lot of people, I started as a developer</h2>
        <p>
          My degree had an iOS specialisation, so that's where I began. My first
          project at TCS was a SwiftUI iPad app for a client. I built the whole
          thing and it shipped in about two months.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then the feature requests dried up and someone handed me the AWS side
          of the same engagement, mostly because there was nothing else for me to
          do. That's the part that stuck. I asked to move.
        </p>

        <h2>What happened after that</h2>
        <p>
          I joined the Platform Solutions unit expecting to build a mobile client
          for TCS Enterprise Manager. Budget said otherwise, and I landed in a
          DevOps role right as the unit decided to fix how it shipped software.
        </p>
        <p>
          What we walked into: 75+ microservices sitting in SVN, deployments
          every Thursday, no review gate, and a revert that took an hour of
          careful manual work. Two of us were told to sort it out.
        </p>
        <p>
          We spent longer than anyone expected just writing down how delivery
          should work before touching Jenkins. Which branches mean what. What a
          merge has to pass. Who's allowed to deploy to prod. It felt slow at the
          time. It was the right call, because every pipeline after that was just
          implementing the document.
        </p>
        <p>
          The revert pipeline is the bit I'm proudest of. Getting it under thirty
          seconds is what actually changed how people behaved — once undoing a
          release stopped being frightening, nobody wanted to wait for Thursday
          any more.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since then it's mostly been databases and access. Superset in front of
          DEV, UAT and prod so nobody needs raw credentials. Bytebase handing out
          logins that expire on their own. An artifact registry decision I'd
          happily defend in an interview. A lot of Terraform.
        </p>

        <h2>Books</h2>
        <p>
          I read constantly, and it's the fastest way to get a sense of how I
          think. Mostly non-fiction and systems writing, with science fiction
          when I want to stop thinking about work entirely.
          My <a href={site.socials.goodreads} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Goodreads</a> is
          public — what I'm reading now, what I've finished, and what I gave up
          on. If you're hiring, it'll tell you more than a skills list will. If
          you just want a recommendation, I always have one.
        </p>

        <h2>The homelab</h2>
        <p>
          I self-host more than is strictly reasonable. Identity, reverse proxy,
          search, monitoring, media, all sitting behind Cloudflare tunnels with
          the config in version control. Most of it exists because I wanted to
          understand something properly, and breaking it at home costs a lot less
          than breaking it at work.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'm writing Flutter for the product's mobile client at the moment, so I
          haven't entirely left app development behind.
        </p>

        <hr class="hair my-10" />

        <h2>Come say hello</h2>
        <p>
          I'm open to DevOps, SRE, platform and cloud architecture roles. I'm
          also happy to talk about self-hosting setups, or argue about books.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="space-y-4 lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:self-start">
        <div class="card p-5">
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">Arc</h2>
          <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3">
            {arc.map((event) => (
              <li class="flex gap-3 text-xs leading-relaxed">
                <span class="shrink-0 font-mono font-semibold" style="color: var(--c-accent);">{event.year}</span>
                <span style="color: var(--c-text-dim);">{event.label}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-5">
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">Education</h2>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm font-medium" style="color: var(--c-text);">{education.degree}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs" style="color: var(--c-text-dim);">{education.specialisation}</p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs" style="color: var(--c-text-dim);">{education.institution}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-xs" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">
            {education.period} · {education.grade}
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5">
            {education.coursework.map((course) => (
              <li class="rounded border px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[0.68rem]"
                  style="border-color: var(--c-border); color: var(--c-text-faint);">
                {course}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card p-5">
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.12em]" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">Connect &amp; follow</h2>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2.5 text-sm">
            {connect.map((item) => (
              <li>
                <a
                  href={item.href}
                  target={item.href.startsWith('mailto:') ? undefined : '_blank'}
                  rel="noopener"
                  class="group flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3 transition-colors"
                  style="color: var(--c-text-dim);"
                >
                  <span class="font-medium" style="color: var(--c-info);">{item.label}</span>
                  <span class="font-mono text-[0.68rem]" style="color: var(--c-text-faint);">{item.handle}</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
